special_abilities:
  - name: "Sees Bad Medicine"
    description: "Senses and actually sees the magic aura of bad medicine from witchcraft and necromancy: objects charged or cursed with it, the bites of magic snakes, people enchanted by witchcraft, witches themselves, victims of possession and of magic mind control. Automatic; no P.P.E."
  - name: "Recognize Werebeast and Evil Spirit Footprints"
    description: "Recognizes the footprints of werebeasts and other evil spirits (supernatural monsters and demons). 30% +5% per level of experience."
  - name: "Recognize Mind Control"
    description: "Recognizes mind control and whether it comes from possession, psionics, magic, hypnosis or drugs. 30% +5% per level of experience."
  - name: "Recognize Bio-Wizardry, Rune Weapons and Techno-Wizardry"
    description: "Recognizes bio-wizardry, real rune weapons and techno-wizard devices. 25% +5% per level of experience."
  - name: "Impervious to Magic Snakes"
    description: "The bite of a witch''s magic snake does him no damage."
  - name: "Impervious to Possession"
    description: "Impervious to possession of any kind. The accompanying +2 to save vs all forms of mind control is carried in bonuses."
  - name: "Make Medicine Stick"
    description: "A 48-hour ritual of fasting, meditation, chanting and help from his guardian spirit, costing 800 P.P.E. The stick is a mace-like scepter: 2D6 S.D.C., double damage to witches, 4D6 M.D. to werebeasts, evil spirits and mega-damage creatures, and smashing an object charged with bad medicine discharges it harmlessly. While he holds it he is impervious to the witch''s evil medicine (the witch''s only) and +1 to parry, and it is indestructible in his hands. Anyone else holding it is +2 to save vs possession and mind control, +2 vs disease and poison, +4 vs horror factor, fear and illusions, and +10% vs coma/death; he often lends it. If it is destroyed he must make another; if stolen, he must recover it. See the medicine-stick gear row."
  - name: "Make Medicine Horn"
    description: "His most important magic object after the stick, made once (unless lost) from a decorated buffalo horn for 500 P.P.E. A focus for rituals, meditation and spirit summoning, and used to make other charms. Impervious to fire and heat, indestructible in its maker''s hands, and a 50 P.P.E. battery only its maker can draw on, once per 24 hours. It loses its magic when he dies. See the medicine-horn gear row."
  - name: "Make Charms & Amulets"
    description: "Charms stay active for years until their maker deactivates them or they are destroyed. He can make the standard amulets (Rifts Ultimate Edition p.223) and his own: Kifaalu taboo horn (1,200 P.P.E.), Mayembe horns of divining (350), Magic Wings (100), Protection from Disease (310), Protection against the Witch''s Life Drain (220) and Protection against Witches and Witchcraft (80). Each is a gear row with its effects."
  - name: "Protection against Witches: Area Ritual"
    description: "The area version of the witch-protection charm: waving the medicine horn and chanting at each entrance, sprinkling ground horn, then driving the horn into the ground before the main entrance. Witches and their magic snakes cannot enter the area; it lasts one day per level and breaks if the horn is removed (witches, their minions and evil spirits cannot touch it). 160 P.P.E., usually gathered from a crowd of chanting villagers."
  - name: "Witch Lure"
    description: "The medicine horn is left in the open and draws magic snakes and other created or summoned servants of witches to it instead of their targets, where they can be seen and destroyed. 120 P.P.E."
  - name: "Meditation"
    description: "By holding or looking at his medicine horn and humming he enters a trance in which he heals and regains spent P.P.E. twice as fast per hour. Also how he contacts his spirit guardian."
  - name: "Spirit Guardian (Emandwa or Balubaale)"
    description: "After 1D4 minutes of meditation he can see and talk with the spirit of one deceased loved one; only he can see it. In a crisis it may come unbidden or in dreams, and it always appears to warn of immediate danger from a taboo item or area. It warns of witches, evil spirits, bad medicine, danger to himself and a wrong course; identifies motives, possession and mind control, old enemies and good medicine; locates secret passages, drinking water (through the medicine horn), lost children and the safest route; and tells whether a person speaks the truth. It can grant +1 to save vs magic and psionics and +2 vs horror factor (one hour per level), half fatigue and half food and water needs (one day per level), or +10% to one key skill (one hour per level). It unties bonds and picks locks at 75%, one try per melee round, and carries him to safety from drowning. An unknown spirit means bad medicine, and he never follows one."
  - name: "Psychic Sensitive"
    description: "All medicine men are psychic sensitives with eight powers: Clairvoyance, Empathy, Presence Sense, See the Invisible, Sense Evil, Sixth Sense, Psychic Diagnosis and Mind Block. I.S.P. is the M.E. attribute number plus 12, +1D6+1 per level."
  - name: "Learning New Spells"
    description: "Additional spells of any level can be learned or purchased at any time regardless of experience, but medicine men are satisfied with their expertise and never learn more than ten spells in all."
  - name: "Ceremonies"
    description: "Chant rituals are otherwise the priest''s, but he knows the Chant to Control Ley Line Storms (110 P.P.E. for him), the Dance to Close a Dimensional Rift, and conducts the Chant of Exorcism and the penance ritual that begins removing a taboo. In the Dance to Chase Away Evil Spirits & Witches and the Dance of Life the priest leads and a priest or medicine man, whichever is more experienced, may channel the magic. A priest may appoint him during a ceremony to draw on the participants'' P.P.E."
restrictions:
  - "Attribute requirements: I.Q., M.A. and M.E. 12 or higher; a high P.E. is ideal but not required."
  - "Weapons are limited to the medicine stick, medicine horn, a knife and one additional weapon of choice. 70% also have an energy pistol and/or rifle or other high-tech weapon with several E-clips."
  - "May wear light mega-damage armor, especially magic types; seldom wears armor during ceremonies."
  - "Cybernetics: starts with none and avoids them because they interfere with magic. Only cybernetic bio-systems for health reasons will be considered."
  - "Also starts with 4D6x1000 credits'' worth of valuables such as gems, gold and livestock, over and above the coin in starting_money."
  - "Typically only high level characters (7th and up) have a Kifaalu horn."
extraction_notes: |
  - Africa printed 79-82 (cache p080-p083): the class intro, O.C.C. abilities
    and powers 1 begin on printed 79; powers 2-11 on 80; the spirit guardian
    list and charms on 81; the area charm, witch lure and the whole O.C.C.
    block (requirements, skills, equipment, armor, money, cybernetics) on 82.
    Printed 81-82 rendered; the text layer''s "3 10" and "1 3" are 310 and 13.
    Ceremonies read on printed 86-89 (cache p087-p090).
  - XP: the African Medicine Man ladder on printed 160, read off a render;
    lower bounds stored as printed.
  - GROUP: the class sits among Africa''s men of magic (medicine man, rain
    maker, priest); no S.D.C. formula is printed, only a +20 S.D.C. bonus.
    occ_group magic, men_of_arms false. The +20 S.D.C. is a pool bonus.
  - BONUSES: +1 to save vs magic of all kinds -> spell_magic 1 and
    ritual_magic 1; +6 horror factor; +1 M.A., M.E. and P.E.; +2 vs mind
    control (power 6) stored unconditionally. The medicine stick''s +1 parry
    and other holder bonuses are conditional and are prose.
  - PSIONICS: eight named sensitive powers granted; type major (a psychic
    sensitive with eight powers, judgement). Psychic Diagnosis is a Healing
    power and Mind Block a Physical one in the catalog; the book calls all
    eight sensitive powers. I.S.P. M.E. + 12, +1D6+1 per level.
  - MAGIC: the fixed starting spells (Globe of Daylight, Ignite Fire, See the
    Invisible, Sense Evil, Armor of Ithan) plus "the taboo and remove taboo
    spells/rituals" granted by name: Bad Medicine: Taboo (the spell''s text on
    printed 77 says a witch or medicine man can make a taboo) and the Remove
    Taboo rite. The Remove Taboo rite''s own note on printed 87 calls it
    exclusive to the rain maker, while the same entry says a medicine man
    conducts its penance and the O.C.C. says he starts with it; granted
    (judgement). Rites granted: Control Ley Line Storms (exclusive to rain
    maker and medicine man), Close a Dimensional Rift (all three know it),
    Exorcism (usually conducted by a medicine man). Chase Away Evil Spirits
    and Dance of Life are led by the priest with the medicine man only
    channelling, so they are not granted and are in the Ceremonies ability.
    spells_starting 0: the book gives no free picks; more can be bought any
    time, ten spells total, which is prose. No spell_traditions_allowed.
  - SKILLS: base = catalog base + printed bonus. Lore: Monsters & Demons ->
    Lore: Demons & Monsters; Lore: Faerie Folk -> Lore: Faeries & Creatures
    of Magic; Basic Math -> Mathematics: Basic; Track Animals -> Track & Trap
    Animals. Native tongue 98% as Language: Native Tongue plus Literacy:
    Native Language at 98. Two languages +20% as two Language: Other picks.
  - RELATED: the book calls them other skills; six, plus two at level 3 and
    one at 6, 9 and 12, from the list printed under Secondary Skills.
    Horsemanship granted beside Pilot Any. SECONDARY: "four at levels one,
    five and ten" read as four at each (judgement); the other reading is four
    spread across those levels.
  - EQUIPMENT: medicine stick and medicine horn (he makes both) and a knife.
    1D4+2 charms stored as three choices among the three protection charms
    (the minimum roll); the remaining 1D4-1, the Kifaalu horn (usually 7th
    level and up), the weapon of choice and the 70% energy weapon are prose.
    Travelling clothes -> clothing, ceremonial clothes -> ceremonial-robe;
    the ceremonial headdress, herbs and medicines and personal items have no
    row. A few sacks as two small sacks. 40 feet of rope -> rope (per 40 ft).
    Food for a month -> four week-long food rations. The riding animal and
    vehicles are prose (lore).
  - MONEY: 2D6x1000 credits stored; 4D6x1000 in valuables (gems, gold,
    livestock) is not coin and is a restriction line.
---

## Lore

The African medicine man is the most esteemed figure in his society after the king: keeper and maker of good medicine, healer, counsellor and above all witch hunter and protector of his people. Most are courteous, perceptive and do not overcharge for their help. Most are native Africans, and most are men, though neither is required.

A medicine man comes to the calling by heredity, by showing an aptitude, or both. An elder takes the youngster, usually 7 to 16 years old, under his wing for 1D4+6 years of study, a bond much like father and son. A small village usually has one medicine man; a large tribe may have a dozen, led by one or two elders, and they work alongside the priests rather than competing with them. Some settle in one village; others wander, helping anyone in need and hunting evil.

His days are spent advising people on medicine, magic and matters of the heart, tending the sick, helping priests prepare ceremonies and telling stories. His four great aims are to help others with his magic, to protect the innocent from evil spirits and bad medicine, to protect them from witches, and to hunt down and destroy witches and demons. He is part detective, part witch hunter and part destroyer of the demonic, knowledgeable in bad medicine, witchcraft, monsters and evil spirits, and his particular skill is identifying the trouble so the right protection can be raised.

## GM Notes

African witches, vampires and entities are his natural enemies, and every evil supernatural being has reason to fear him. Men of science suggest his spirit guardian is self-hypnosis and psychic intuition; the medicine man laughs at the idea. Young medicine men usually start with a fair riding animal or on foot; experienced and wealthy ones may have a fine mount, a vehicle and pack animals.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'medicine-man');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'medicine-man';

